\begin{abstract}
End-to-end (E2E) simulators play a central role in the development, verification, and validation of Earth observation mission preformance within ESA.
Despite their importance, architectural aspects of E2E simulation software design though-process are sparsely documented and not discussed within literatue, which in turn can lead to suboptimal design choices.
Implementation details are often not abstracted and mission-specific, making reuse, testing, and scaling costly. This paper provides a concise architectural review of current E2E simulation practices
used in ESA-like Earth observation context, focusing on software structure, development phases, and operational limitations.
Key functional and non-functional requirements are identified, together with common pain points that affect cost, reproducibility, and testability.
A comparison is drawn with alternative E2E simulation practices employed in large-scale scientific software systems, such as the ATLAS Athena framework.
Based on these observations, the paper outlines a lightweight, Python-centric architectural direction and provides pragmatic recommendations aimed
at simplifying E2E simulator development and reducing long-term maintenance and verification effort. In the paper, one of alternative approaches is examined as it was implemented for CHIME E2E Instrument Optical Module.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

End-to-end (E2E) mission performance simulators (E2ES) are indispensable tools in the development of modern Earth observation (EO) missions at the European Space Agency (ESA) \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025, Sgheri_FORUM_E2ES_2021, SanjuanFerrer_ROSEL_E2E_2024, biblos_gmv_2020}. These simulators are critical for assessing mission performance and verifying ground processing chains \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025, Sgheri_FORUM_E2ES_2021, SanjuanFerrer_ROSEL_E2E_2024}. To ensure interoperability, ESA has successfully standardized the high-level architecture of these systems, particularly regarding module interfaces and the overarching framework provided by openSF \cite{ESA_Generic_E2E_ICD_2023, openSF_ICD_2018, biblos_gmv_2020, EO_E2E2_RefArch_2018}. \\

\noindent
However, while external interfaces are well-defined, there is a significant lack of consolidated guidance regarding the internal implementation of these modules. Aspects such as coding style, library selection, and internal software architecture are often left to the discretion of individual contractors, with few recommendations including OpenMP and OpenCL \cite{biblos_gmv_2020, ESA_Generic_E2E_Req_2022}. This lack of standardization frequently results in non-cohesive codebases that rely on suboptimal calculation strategies or redundant internal functions rather than modern scientific libraries. Consequently, many existing simulators suffer from a lack of abstraction, making them difficult to scale, test, or adapt to new mission requirements. \\

\noindent
These implementation shortcomings have practical consequences beyond software maintenance. The high complexity and lack of transparency in the code create a hard work enviorment for Instrument Performance engineers(I'm taking this from Whitepaper, is this corrent possition name?). These specialists, who often have backgrounds in physics rather than software engineering, are responsible for validating the underlying scientific algorithms \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}. When implementation details are obscured by overly complex software implementation, the gap between the physical model and its digital twin widens, increasing the risk of errors and driving up development costs.\\

\noindent
The goal of this paper is to review current implemenataion and technological practices in the ESA EO context, identify core requirements and limitations, and propose simplifications aimed at reducing development and testing effort. Building upon existing ESA guidelines and the BIBLOS framework \cite{ESA_Generic_E2E_Req_2022, biblos_gmv_2020, biblos3_esr_2023}, this work incorporates insights from recent ESA white papers and internal discussions. We compare current practices with alternative large-scale scientific frameworks, such as ATLAS Athena at CERN, to identify transferable lessons. Finally, we propose a Python-centric architectural direction designed to produce cleaner, more readable code that serves as its own documentation and support multi-hardware options, ultimately facilitating easier validation for ESA staff and more efficient delivery for industrial contractors.

