\section{Background and Scope}

To establish a common baseline, it is necessary to define the functional boundaries of the systems under discussion. According to the ESA E2E White Paper \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}, an End-to-End Mission Performance Simulator (E2ES) is defined as:

\begin{quote}
    \textit{"...chains of software tools developed to simulate the generation of the geophysical observables target scene, the whole observation process from space including orbit, pointing and guidance, the instruments, the on-board data generation and the processing of the outputs of these instruments to retrieve the estimate of the original observables."}
\end{quote}

In this context, the E2ES serves as a "digital twin" of the entire measurement chain. While the high-level orchestration of these chains is often handled by frameworks like openSF, this paper focuses specifically on the \textbf{module-level architecture}—the internal design and implementation of the individual building blocks (e.g., Instrument Simulation Modules) that constitute the chain.

\subsection{Scope of the Discussion}
The scope of this work is centered on the software engineering and operational aspects of E2ES development rather than the underlying physics. Specifically, we address:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Software Architecture and Technology: Reviewing the current use of object oriented module achitecture, languages and libraries, with a focus on moving toward a python-centric stack that utilizes modern scientific computing tools developed in performent languages.
    \item Hardware Utilization: Discussing the trade-offs between CPU-based processing and GPU-accelerated workflows to meet increasing performance demands.
    \item Validation and Testing Workflows: Proposing methods for automated error reporting and result analysis to replace labor-intensive manual verification and properly analysing numerical errors.
    \item Documentation Practices: Exploring the transition from static, text-heavy implementation documentation toward self-documenting code.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
Conversely, this paper does not cover mission-specific algorithm derivations or detailed physical modeling for particular Earth observation sensors. \\

\noindent
Finally, the paper references a practical implementation case: the Instrument Optical Module for the CHIME (Copernicus Hyperspectral Imaging Mission for the Environment) E2ES. While this module did not adhere to all the proposed guidelines from its inception, the lessons learned during its development served as the catalyst for the requirements and architectural directions proposed in the following sections.

\subsection{Target Audience}
The recommendations provided herein are intended for software implementation contractors Scientific Algorithms’ developers, Instrument Performance engineers, Simulator and Processor Engineers and Ground Segment System engineers. We want to provide tools for industry and expected standard for ESA Staff. By lowering the implementation complexity, we aim to provide ESA staff with more transparent tools for verification while reducing the development burden on industrial partners. 

