\subsection{Common Development Phases}

The development of E2ES software follows an evolutionary approach that spans the entire mission lifecycle, progressing from simplified feasibility models to detailed, mission-specific implementations \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}. This progression can be characterized by several distinct stages:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Initial Generic Implementation (Phase 0/A/B1):} A generic simulator is developed to assess mission feasibility and L2 retrieval concepts. This early version typically employs simplified error models applied to Scene Generation outputs \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}.

    \item \textbf{Mission-Specific Development (Phase B2/C/D/E1):} The generic foundation is expanded into detailed, mission-specific implementations. Separate ATBDs are developed for individual modules (ISM, L1 processing, L2 retrieval), often by different industrial consortia. Software is then implemented according to these algorithmic specifications \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}.

    \item \textbf{Validation and Test Data Generation:} Throughout development, Test Data Sets (TDS) are created for systematic verification and validation of both individual modules and the integrated chain \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}.

    \item \textbf{On-Ground Calibration Support:} The E2ES supports On-Ground Calibration Activities, where measured data is used for characterizing instrument behavior and preparing final IOM module \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}. (Question, what phase is OGC?, also I think this may need a bit of rephrasing)

    \item \textbf{Operational Ground Processing (Phase E2):} The toolchain transitions to supporting Ground Processor maintenance, algorithm evolution, and anomaly investigation during long-term operations \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}.
\end{enumerate}

This phased approach underscores the importance of maintainable, testable software architectures. The need for rapid algorithm iteration during development and subsequent long-term operational use creates tension between flexibility and performance—a challenge that motivates the architectural considerations discussed in later sections. \\

\noindent
At this point, it is important to recognize the computational nature of E2ES software. In most cases, these are image processing applications that handle very large datasets. Given that processing images of this scale is both memory-intensive and computationally demanding, these modules are typically implemented in highly performant C/C++ \cite{biblos_gmv_2020, EO_E2E2_RefArch_2018, ESA_Generic_E2E_Req_2022}, with parallelization support provided through OpenMP and OpenCL \cite{ESA_Generic_E2E_Req_2022, biblos_gmv_2020}.

