\subsection{Recuring limitations and pain points}

While the requirements outlined in Section 3.3 are understandable from a performance perspective, they often impose limitations that are not aligned with practical development realities. Performance is not the only consideration—development cost, ease of maintenance, and accessibility to domain experts are equally important. This disconnect is particularly evident in environments where ATBD preparation, software development, and validation are distributed across different locations and split between ESA and industrial partners \cite{ESA_E2E_WhitePaper_2025}. \\

\noindent
Several recurring challenges have been identified through recent mission developments and internal discussions, notably within the CHIME and LSTM teams, who have advocated for more flexible technological approaches, especially in context of Instrument Simulator:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Language and Ecosystem Constraints:} Python is currently the most popular programming language, with extensive support for scientific computing and a rich ecosystem of data science tools \cite{python_popularity, cern_collumnar_analysis_Feickert_2025}. While ground processing chains may require peak performance, achieving comparable performance for Instrument Simulation Modules (ISM) or Scene Generation Modules (SGM) using Python with modern libraries would significantly reduce development costs. This is especially relevant given that many system experts routinely use Python but lack deep C++ expertise.

    \item \textbf{Underutilization of GPU Acceleration:} Although requirement E2E-ENV-GEN-110 mentions GPU support, real implementations often lack this capability despite the potential for orders-of-magnitude performance improvements. This was evident in early testing of a simple per-pixel uncertainty estimation pipeline for the LSTM and CHIME L1C products at ESA, where preliminary results showed processing time reductions of 3–4 orders of magnitude(not accounting for host-device data transfer) when comparing GPU processing (H100) to CPU processing (16-core Intel Xeon). While GPU usage can be limited by PCIe memory bandwidth \cite{image_processing_hardware_review_2024}, most use cases do not involve excessive data transfer and could benefit substantially from GPU-accelerated image processing \cite{perfromance_gpu_conv_2010, image_processing_hardware_review_2024}. Providing both CPU and GPU execution paths would offer flexibility for different operational scenarios, particularly when breakpoint file generation is not required during nominal runs.

    \item \textbf{Inadequate Performance Monitoring:} Current implementations lack automated support for timeliness metrics and performance visualization. Simple, automated timing reports—supported by both C++ and Python—could facilitate performance assessment.

    \item \textbf{Manual Validation and Error Analysis:} The absence of automated report generation for results analysis and numerical error propagation(which is completly unaccounted for) places unnecessary burden on validation teams. Automated tooling for these tasks would reduce manual effort and improve reproducibility.

    \item \textbf{Hardware-Software Co-Design Neglect:} The hardware architecture of ground processing systems is as critical as the software implementation itself. Fast memory interfaces, optimized I/O strategies, and detached save threads could yield significant performance improvements but are rarely considered in current architectural discussions.

    \item \textbf{Complexity Barriers for Domain Experts:} Overly complex implementations at the ISM level hinder the productivity of Instrument Performance engineers and system experts, who are responsible for validating the underlying physics. Simpler, more readable implementations—without sacrificing modeling accuracy—would enable these experts to work more efficiently.

    \item \textbf{Documentation-Implementation Gap:} Traditional documentation can diverge from actual implementation, introducing validation risks. Self-documenting code—where clear structure, meaningful naming, and inline comments serve as the primary documentation—could eliminate this discrepancy. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) for code analysis further support this approach, as LLMs can more easily parse and validate algorithmic correctness directly from well-written code, and could compare it to ATBD. While LLMs are already being explored at ESA for management and information tasks, they could also be leveraged to cross-check implementation against ATBD specifications, provided the code itself is sufficiently readable.

    \item \textbf{Standardization and Version Control:} Standards for output formats, configuration files, and interface specifications are often scattered across multiple documents. Consolidating these in a common repository—complete with examples used for automated testing—would ensure that all stakeholders have access to the most recent, unified specifications. More systematic use of version control platforms such as GitLab would further enhance traceability and collaboration.
\end{enumerate}

These limitations collectively increase development time, reduce code transparency, and complicate validation efforts. Addressing them requires a shift toward more pragmatic architectural choices that balance performance with usability and long-term maintainability.

