As part of a recent internship with the CHIME team, a prototype Instrument Optical Module (IOM) was developed using a Python-based stack centered on Dask, xarray, NumPy, and Numba. The objective was to provide a more flexible alternative to existing C++ implementations, particularly for supporting On-Ground Calibration Activities. The architecture emphasized readability and maintainability through a functional programming approach across all implementation levels.

\subsection{Observed Strengths}
The prototype demonstrated several advantages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Memory Efficiency Through Chunking:} Dask's native chunking support enabled significant reductions in RAM requirements. Initial input images exceeding 50 GB—which could drive total memory consumption beyond 128 GB during processing—were successfully handled on virtual machines with only 32 GB of RAM. This capability would enable simulator deployment on standard computing infrastructure rather than requiring specialized high-memory systems.

    \item \textbf{Algorithm Clarity:} Implementation using NumPy and scientific Python libraries resulted in substantially shorter and more transparent code compared to equivalent C++ implementations.

    \item \textbf{Simplified Data Management:} Dask's lazy evaluation model streamlined checkpoint (BKP) file generation, consolidating multi-step save operations into single function calls.

    \item \textbf{Comparable Performance:} Preliminary benchmarks on a representative test case showed processing times of 60–105 minutes for the Python implementation versus approximately 75 minutes for the nominal C++ version, suggesting performance parity for certain workloads. However, more comprehensive testing across diverse scenarios is required for validation.

    \item \textbf{Extensibility:} Performance-critical functions can be selectively reimplemented in C++ or CUDA and integrated via Pybind11, enabling targeted optimization while preserving Python's high-level expressiveness.
\end{enumerate}

The OGC support was done similary to Athena configuration, where the in facto configuration file for OGC is the additional runner file which manages the configuraiton \cite{athena_configuration}. This approach is videly used, as allows for python extensibility and data analysis on top of it.

\subsection{Identified Limitations}
Several challenges emerged during development:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Error Diagnostics:} Dask generates verbose stack traces that can obscure the root cause of failures, complicating debugging efforts.
    
    \item \textbf{Backend Instabilities:} The netCDF4 and HDF5 backends occasionally failed when parallel workers attempted concurrent writes, requiring workarounds with zarr.
    
    \item \textbf{Overhead and Control:} Python's multiprocessing layer introduces overhead and limits low-level execution control compared to native parallelization frameworks like OpenMP.
\end{enumerate}

In general, this experiment suggested that especially with specific simple high performace library writen with C++ and CUDA and some utilities included, the development of the simulator could be vastly sped up, providing all the support needed.\\

