\subsection{Implications and Future Direction}
This prototype demonstrates the viability of Python-based implementations for ISM-level modules. A purpose-built library combining Python's scientific ecosystem with selective C++/CUDA backends for performance-critical operations could accelerate development while maintaining adequate performance and improving code transparency for domain experts. \\

\noindent
For ground processing chains handling smaller datasets, emphasis should shift from chunking to GPU acceleration and optimized I/O. A unified library supporting both CPU and GPU paths, with standardized profiling and automated error reporting, would address key limitations identified in Section 3.4. \\

\noindent
The CHIME IOM prototype establishes a proof of concept for Python-centric E2ES architectures. Further work is needed to develop robust, production-ready frameworks. \\

\section{Discussion and Recommendations}
This section synthesizes the findings of the review and comparison.
Architectural trade-offs between existing and alternative approaches are discussed, with emphasis on simplicity,
testability, and reproducibility.
Practical recommendations are provided for the design and evolution of E2E simulators in ESA Earth observation workflows,
aimed at reducing development and verification cost.
Limitations of the presented analysis and directions for future work are briefly outlined.

Recommendations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Quick and simple support for good timeliness metrics. This could be standardized and printed by the timer objects. Both C++ and python support that.
    \item Results analysis through automated report generation.
    \item Automated Numerical error report generation.
    \item Initial tests for modules clearly suggest the vast time difference given by the access to the GPU for processing. While in file save heavy situation it may not be super optimal due to GPU VRAM -> memory bandwidth limintation \cite{}, hardware techniques exists to speed that up \cite{}. In our case it is best to provide user with both options, as often for preliminary scientific usecase BKP saving is not necessary.
    \item In cases of fast delivery time needed, for example fire prevention for america, it is likely cheaper and more robust to not provide on satelite data analysis and AI algorithm, but do that early on in optimized processing toolchain at L1C, which with good optimal GPU implementation would be matter of seconds not tens of minutes. 
    \item The hardware of the Ground processing is as important as software implementation, fast memmory for initial reading, perhaps even full on RAM interfaces with detached save threads could be of very significant improvment.
    \item Simplicity of implemenetaiton at ISM level will lead to higher output of work of system engineers mentiond in the document.
    \item Not simpler modeling, but simpler implementation and readability of that modeling.
    \item Documentation in latex and usage of LLMs for automated algorithm validation. There is significant effort now at ESA in EO to use LLMs for managment and information purpouses, but in general, LLMs could also be used to ensure algorithic corectness as code is the easiest thing that can be parsed by LLM. The algorithimc ATBD shall also be updated.
    \item One way to do the goal is have next in depth ATBD of code, but what if we can make the code document itself? Then we can eliminate the error that could happen between documentation-implementation.
    \item The goal is not to avoid big run funstion with hundret's of lines, but provide ones that are clear to understand and use multiline function signatures when it helps with code readablity.
\end{itemize}

\section{Internal Physical Modeling Suggestions}
The problem of simplifyling how the instrument optical module is modeled does not really seem to be very crucial. ATBD of IOM is not particularly complex, few non-linear effects are modeled at detector or FEE level, while rest, with exception of smile and keystone and transission from electrons into DN, are quiet simple, and are either one of those cathegories:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 1D resampling
    \item 1D convolution for 2D list of arrays.
    \item 2D convolution for 1D list of arrays.
    \item Element wise mutiplication for one of dimentions.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
This however always includes one of two things, either 1D resampling(linear interpolation from one system to other) or preparation of scene for always same initial grid of $[25.6, 25.4 ..., 0.0, ..., 25.6]$ with different margins.\\

\noindent
My suggestion would be at this point, to prepare some abstracted function which does that, and always does that with same margin, most of those functions don't really matter if we include 1 or 2 margin pixels physicly, or at least there is no way that it should matter, in case it does, that means that we are assuming non 0 out of scope that is measured, meaning this could acctualy lead to error. This could contribute to some non zero(likely much less then 1\%) error, it may even be that this is what is happening at telescope lsf, as we had some differences at border without margin, which would not make sense. \\

\noindent
Current version of OPSI resamples image as first step of detector, however this resampling does not really matter if we do it before Motion module. As SGM outputs some arbitrary image, spectral sampling, it can actually be resampled to one that matches oversampled binned scene as first thing in box, then we can just avoid the convolution with delta things all together, which would actually simplify the beviour of system vastly, as the function responsible for convolution and resampling is worst piece of code in there, which does some heavy level magic to get which indexes it should convolve around. \\

\noindent
So I would agree, that perhaps those few simple steps and just cleaner code would be easiest way to simplify the behaviour, the subset of physical modeling that is comperhesible to me is not that hard, and hard part is simple to implement and explained in ATBD. It is just that implementation is hard to understand often due to margin(which should not matter but matter) and how resamplings are done, and how the new samplings are prepared. Those can actually lead to huge errors which look good extreamly quickly. Make samplings simple again!

